<html><body><p>The bank’s “prudent” stance — and the word is doing a lot of work here — cost shareholders about £40 million.</p><p>“We got it wrong,” the chair said, in the closest thing to an apology anyone has heard from a regulated institution since 2008. “We’re sorry.”</p><p style="font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:16px;line-height:24px;">Underlying yields rose for the fourth straight session. That matters because everything in the last two years has been priced off the assumption that borrowing costs had peaked. Four straight up days in the long bond is the market starting to doubt that assumption out loud. The energy complex was the other tell. Crude sold off hard into the London close, then quietly recovered in the American afternoon. Nobody at the exchanges will say why, and the volume profile suggests a single large seller rather than a change in fundamentals.</p></body></html>